 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S441-021\", \"clauses\": {\"describe_position\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"The {objects_plural} are placed in a row. One {object_singular} is number {n} in the row. Which word describes its position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F15 asks the learner to describe the position of a named object in a row using an ordinal word, which is the task named by the clause.\"}, \"objects\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F15\", \"template\": \"The {objects_plural} are placed in a row. One {object_singular} is number {n} in the row. Which word describes its position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F15 prints the objects (object_singular and objects_plural drawn from the generator's own object lists) placed in the row.\"}, \"ordinal_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F17\", \"template\": \"What ordinal describes position number {n}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F17 names the ordinal concept directly and asks for the ordinal for a position number from 1 to 4 or 6 to 9 (observed runs).\"}, \"1st\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints symbol values that include 1st, so a problem can address the 1st range clause. The keyed answer for this template is drawn from F31, which is itself failing (see failing_families), so the key must be fixed before this clause's items are trusted.\"}, \"2nd\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints symbol values that include 2nd. The key-source caveat in the 1st entry applies here as well.\"}, \"3rd\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints symbol values that include 3rd. The key-source caveat in the 1st entry applies here as well.\"}, \"up_to_10th\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F19\", \"template\": \"Which word describes the {symbol} position?\", \"reasoning\": \"F19 prints 10th among its symbol values, so the up to 10th clause can be addressed. The key-source caveat in the 1st entry applies here as well.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F03\", \"F12\", \"F14\", \"F16\", \"F20\", \"F21\", \"F22\", \"F23\", \"F24\", \"F25\", \"F31\", \"F32\", \"F33\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"'{capitalize}' is an {ord_label} word.\", \"The number {n} in order is called the {symbol} ({word}).\", \"The {lineup} are lined up on a shelf from left to right. Which one is in {symbol} place?\", \"The {symbol} ({word}) place is position number {n}.\", \"Who arrived earlier: the student in {symbol} place or {symbol2} place?\", \"Write the symbol for the {word} position.\", \"{capitalize} = position {n}, written as {symbol}.\", \"{head} and {last}\", \"{lineup} stand in a row. Who is {symbol} in the row?\", \"{n}{v}\", \"{w}\", \"{answer}\", \"{member}\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"Several families can produce wrong or out-of-scope text. (1) F31 (template '{w}') draws its word from _ORDINAL_WORDS, which includes eleventh through twentieth. Those words are beyond the lc's range (1st to 10th, student_vocab) and the family is unlinked to any symbol or n, so it can key a word that does not match the position asked. F03 and F22 print F31 through their capitalize slot, so they inherit the out-of-scope words. (2) F25 (template '{n}{v}') has its suffix slot as an independent choice from the ordinal suffixes with no link to n, so it can print malformed ordinals; it is used in correct_answer, distractors and options. F20 prints F25 in symbol2. (3) F12, F16, F21 and F22 use the word slot, which has no derivation in value_derivations tying it to symbol or n. F12 and F16 can state a symbol and a word that do not name the same place; F21 can key a symbol that does not match the word asked; F22 can equate a word with a position and symbol that do not match. These are misleading statements in hints or keyed answers. (4) F20 (symbol2 from F25, F32 and F33) can print object or name text inside a position comparison, and F33 and F32 offer names and objects as positions or keyed answers. (5) F23 (head from F26 to F29, last from the full pool) mixes people and objects in one lineup, and last is not declared distinct from head, so a name can repeat. F14 and F24 print that lineup: F14 prints 'The' before a mixed or name lineup, and F24 asks 'Who' about a lineup that may consist of objects. Families F06, F08, F09, F13, F15, F17, F18, F19 and F30 were checked and are not themselves at fault, but F19's keyed answer depends on F31, so F19 is cited for clause coverage with that caveat. F01 prints 'order word', which is not in the lc's curriculum_vocab, and was not judged failing. The overall verdict is FAIL.\"}",
